WHAT WAS MEASURED
-----------------
One deterministic binary-heavy workload, run twice. The single variable is
whether `Process::increase_virtual_binary_heap` (`process/mod.rs:396`) is
called when a ProcBin is placed on a process heap.

  arm A  "as-is"         no increment — what production does at e168115
  arm B  "trigger-live"  increment by the ProcBin's off-heap byte count

Workload: 2048 ProcBin allocations per arm. 8 processes; two heap
configurations (233-word default nursery x4 processes, 32768-word nursery x4)
so the result is not an artefact of the small default heap. 256 allocations per
process, byte sizes cycling [65, 512, 4096, 65536] — all strictly above
`REFC_BINARY_THRESHOLD` (64, `term/shared_binary.rs:18`), so every one is a
refcounted ProcBin. One in four is retained as a live root in an x register
(rolling 16-slot window); the rest are dropped. No wall clock, no RNG: every
number is a count or a word/byte total.

ARM B IS SEAM-NEUTRAL. No production file was modified — `p4-production.patch`
is 0 bytes, the literal `git --no-pager diff --no-ext-diff` output. The
increment is made from the probe program through the already-public increment
API. See SEAM-AMBIGUITY.md for why, and for the candidate seam list.


HOW GC INVOCATIONS WERE COUNTED (no new production instrumentation)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Derived exactly from pre-call process state, per `gc/mod.rs:135-176`:

  alloc(p,w):      if !pressure(p) { if available() >= w -> return }   no GC
                   ensure_space(p, w, 256)
  ensure_space:    if available() >= w && !pressure(p) { return }      no GC
                   collect_minor_with_live(...)                        GC

Over the four cases of (pressure, available() >= w) a collection runs iff
`pressure || available() < w`. The probe computes that from public accessors
only, before each call, and lets the real `gc::alloc` do the work.

A second, independent signal corroborates it: young-generation residency not
advancing by exactly the allocation, or a region capacity changing. The two
agree EXACTLY in both arms (12 and 12; 744 and 744).


