  arm B  69,696 bytes (default-233) / 565,312 bytes (large-32768)


WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
--------------------
1. At e168115 the vheap trigger contributes NOTHING. Arm A forced zero
   collections through `virtual_binary_pressure_exceeds_heap`; all 12 of its
   collections were ordinary "nursery is full" events. On the 32768-word heap
   arm A collected ZERO times in 1024 allocations. The predicate degenerates to
   `heap_used >= heap_capacity`, exactly as the brief pins it.

2. Turning the increment on multiplies GC invocations by 62x on this workload,
   and every one of the additional collections is attributable to the pressure
   predicate alone (`by_no_nursery_room` falls to 0 in arm B, because the extra
   collections keep the nursery from ever filling).

3. The trigger LATCHES. Once retained-or-promoted ProcBin bytes exceed the
   heap's byte capacity the predicate is permanently true, so every subsequent
   allocation collects. Mechanism, visible in the end state: minor collections
   promote live ProcBins into old space, where a minor walk
   (`gc/mod.rs:282-301`, young region only) can no longer reclaim them. On the
   default-233 processes the end-state `virtual_binary_heap` is 69,696 bytes
   against a total heap capacity of 466 words = 3,728 bytes — 18.7x over the
   latch point, from 4,160 bytes of promoted 65-byte binaries plus one live
   65,536-byte young one. 69.9% of allocations collected as a result.

4. The effect is NOT an artefact of the small default nursery. On the
   32768-word heap the count still moves from 0 to 28.

5. The increment does do what a pacing counter is supposed to do: end-state
   heap residency falls 53.5% (3948 -> 1836 words). The cost of that is item 2.

6. Wiring the increment turns `erlang:memory(binary)` from a constant 0 into a
   real number (2,540,032 bytes here).

DO-NOT-ASSERT (ruled). `gc/tests.rs:604-612` names "the production ~25 GB
residency face". Those walls pass because the test helper `gc/tests.rs:78-87`
performs the increment production omits — confirmed at the bytes. Whether the
production residency face is that link is NOT established, and nothing above
claims it either way. Items 1 and 3 are offered as inputs to that question, not
as an answer to it.


POPULATION COVERAGE OF THE ARM-A ZERO BASELINE
----------------------------------------------
The brief asks whether arm A's zero covers both `erlang:memory(binary)`
populations. Confirmed in context at e168115: it covers BOTH, by construction.

`SharedState::process_heap_and_binary_words` (`scheduler/mod.rs:852-875`) sums
over every non-exited process slot, taking one of two values:

  ProcessSlot::Present(scheduled)  -> `scheduled.0.virtual_binary_heap()`   :864
  ProcessSlot::Executing(metadata) -> `metadata.binary_heap_size`           :868

The ONLY production writer of `metadata.binary_heap_size` is
`scheduler/execution/core.rs:321`, and it writes `process.virtual_binary_heap()`.
The other four assignment sites are test files (`teardown_admission_tests.rs:33`,
`readiness_tests.rs:42`, `scheduler/tests.rs:2015`, `supervision_tests.rs:145`).
There is no other feeder of the snapshot path.

So the two "sources" are ONE counter at two freshness levels. Nothing in
production increments that counter (`increase_virtual_binary_heap` has exactly
one workspace caller, the test helper at `gc/tests.rs:86`), and the field is
initialised to 0 (`process/mod.rs:270`) and otherwise only `saturating_sub`'d.
Arm A's measured 0 is therefore the value for both populations, not a
sampling of one of them.

CORRECTION to the premise as handed down: the snapshot is taken at
schedule-IN, not at deschedule. `scheduler/execution/core.rs:311-334` sits in
`take_runnable_process`, which removes the process from its slot to run it and
installs `ProcessSlot::Executing(metadata)`. `store_runnable_process` (`:344`)
does not write the field at all — on deschedule the slot returns to
`Present`, whose read is live. The staleness window is therefore the whole
execution slice, opening when the process starts running. This does not change
the coverage conclusion; it changes which value is stale and for how long.


